  ],
  "text": "The person who probably saved more lives than anyone in history, and almost nobody knows the name. In the nineteen sixties, serious people were predicting mass famine in India within a decade. Norman Borlaug spent years crossing wheat by hand in Mexico. He was chasing one thing. A wheat that would not fall over. Tall wheat given fertiliser grows heavy heads and collapses under its own weight. So he bred it short. A dwarf wheat that puts the energy into grain instead of stalk. India adopted it, and inside a decade went from famine risk to grain surplus. Be honest about the cost though. It needs heavy fertiliser and a great deal of water. Groundwater has been drawn down hard, and crop diversity has narrowed to almost nothing. He did not end hunger. He bought time. What we do with it is a separate question. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
